    "knowledge_point": "Calculating an Indeterminate Limit of the Form Infinity Divided by Infinity Using L'Hopital's Rule",
    "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}.$",
    "worked_solution": "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{cot}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{-{csc}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}{(\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
